          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            of (regular)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+maps"} rational maps
          %p
            Under good conditions, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} subspace inclusion
            of the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+maps"} rational maps
            (regular, see Rem.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NotionsOfRationalMaps"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifolds"} complex manifolds
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+varieties"} algebraic varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
            = succeed "induces" do
              %br/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            in integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            in low degrees, while for maps out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
            this is even an isomorphism on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            in low degrees:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Maps
                    %mi rat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mi homology
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi iso
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mi in
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi low
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi degree
                  %msub
                    %mi Maps
                    %mi cts
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Maps_{rat}(X_1, X_2)
                  \\xhookrightarrow{ {homology\; iso} \atop {in\; low\; degree} }
                  Maps_{cts}(X_1, X_2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This phenomenon originates in results of
            %a{:href => "#Segal79"} Segal 1979
            and is commonly referred to by Segal’s name (e.g. “theorems of Segal-type” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FriedlanderLawson97"} Friedlander &amp; Lawson 1997, Sec. 5.C
          %p Whenever this holds it provides
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                from left to right:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
                tools for analyzing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
                of rational hypersurfaces;
            %li
              %p
                from right to left: small algebraic models for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
          %p at least up to some dimension.
